  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and Rationale\nThe proposed strategy uses a structured optimization approach with a two-phase process: a global search using a genetic algorithm optimized for the block structure of h, followed by a local refinement using CVX relaxation with MOSEK. This addresses the problem's non-convexity and combinatorial nature, leveraging the efficiency of CVX for smooth optimization after coarsely identifying the solution space.\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise function with:\n- h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0, 1], represented by n_blocks blocks.\n- Variable block_breaks: n_blocks + 1 positions in [0, 2].\n- Variable block_values: n_blocks values in [0, 1].\n\nConstrain:\n1. 0 \u2264 block_values \u2264 1\n2. \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe overlap integral becomes:\nC\u2085 = max\u2096 (sum_{overlap regions} h_i (1 - h_{i+k} )) * dx\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n#### 3.1 Initial Setup\n1. Read initial_h_values (n=70) if available.\n2. Initialize block_breaks at quartiles: [0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2].\n3. Set block_values = [1, 0, 1, 0].\n\n#### 3.2 Genetic Algorithm Search\n1. Create a population of 20 h_block individuals.\n2. Evaluate each with the overlap integral.\n3. Perform crossover and mutation for 500 generations or until budget exhausted.\n\n#### 3.3 CVX Relaxation\n1. Use GA's best as warm start.\n2. Formulate convex problem in CVXPY with MOSEK solver.\n3. Optimize block_values to minimize C\u2085.\n\n#### 3.4 Feasibility Repair\n1. Clip and rescale to ensure 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1 and \u222bh = 1.\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Variations\nStart with 5 blocks, then extend to 10. Use genetic operations with mutation rate 0.1 and crossover rate 0.8.\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nAllocate 60% to GA, 35% to CVX, 5% to repair.\n\n### 6. Validation and Return\nCompute overlap integral, ensure constraints, return (h, C\u2085, n_blocks).",
